# Architecture

```
server.py                    ← FastMCP entry point; auth setup; GitHubClient resolution; 29 MCP tool definitions
src/
├── github_client.py         ← Async REST + GraphQL client (httpx); the only place that calls GitHub API
├── cache.py                 ← Shared repo data TTLCache instances: tree_cache (5 min), file_cache (2 min)
├── auth/
│   ├── __init__.py          ← Exports setup_auth(), parse_bool()
│   ├── provider.py          ← setup_auth(): reads MCP auth env vars, builds MultiAuth provider
│   ├── oauth.py             ← Scope normalization, LoginFilteredGitHubProvider, LoginFilteredGitHubTokenVerifier
│   └── token.py             ← build_token_verifier() factory (DebugTokenVerifier + hmac)
└── tools/
    ├── file_ops.py          ← read_file, list_directory, find_files
    ├── code_search.py       ← search_code, get_file_summary
    ├── git_ops.py           ← list_branches, get_commits, get_diff, get_blame, get_file_history
    ├── repo_info.py         ← get_repo_info, get_repo_structure, get_languages, get_rate_limit
    ├── write_ops.py         ← create_branch, push_files, delete_file, workflow tools
    ├── pr_ops.py            ← create/list/get/merge PRs, add_pr_comment, create_issue
    └── todo_ops.py          ← todo_write, todo_read, todo_update (in-session task tracking)
```

## Two-layer tool pattern

`server.py` contains thin `@mcp.tool()` wrappers that validate nothing -- they call async `_get_client()` and delegate to a pure async function in `src/tools/`. All real logic lives in the tool modules. This keeps tools independently testable without FastMCP.

## GitHubClient

`GitHubClient` wraps:
- REST calls via `self._http` (httpx.AsyncClient with `api.github.com` base URL)
- GraphQL calls via `self._graphql_http` (used only for blame)
- Rate limit tracking -- updated from response headers on every call

Files > 1 MB are fetched via the Git Blobs API (`get_blob`) instead of the Contents API.

## GitHub Token Source

GitHub API access is separate from MCP server authentication.

`GITHUB_TOKEN_MODE` controls which PAT is used for GitHub API calls:

- `env` (default) -- create and validate one server-level `GitHubClient` from `GITHUB_PAT` during lifespan startup.
- `request` -- require each MCP request to send a GitHub PAT in `GITHUB_PAT_HEADER` (`X-GitHub-PAT` by default).
- `request_or_env` -- use the request header first, then fall back to the server-level `GITHUB_PAT`.

`Authorization` is reserved for MCP server authentication and must not be used as `GITHUB_PAT_HEADER`.

## Request GitHubClient Cache

In request modes, `server.py` keeps an `OrderedDict` cache of request-scoped `GitHubClient` instances keyed by SHA-256 of the PAT, never the plaintext token.

- `GITHUB_CLIENT_CACHE_MAX_SIZE` bounds the number of cached request clients (default `128`).
- `GITHUB_CLIENT_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS` closes clients idle past the TTL (default `1800`).
- `GITHUB_VALIDATE_REQUEST_PAT=true` validates a new request PAT with GitHub `/user` before caching.
- Evicted, expired, invalid, and shutdown clients are closed so httpx pools do not leak.
- Cache mutation is protected by an async lock; concurrent first use of the same uncached PAT may perform duplicate validation, but the duplicate client is closed and only one client remains cached.

## Caching

`tree_cache` and `file_cache` in `src/cache.py` are module-level singletons shared across all tool calls. Cache keys use the pattern `"tree:{owner}/{repo}:{ref}"`. TTLCache is acceptable for the async single-process server model.

## Search strategy

`search_code` uses two paths: simple text queries on the default branch go to GitHub's Search API (30 req/min rate limit); regex patterns or branch-specific searches fetch files locally and search with Python `re`.

## Tests

Tests in `tests/` use a shared `mock_client` fixture (`AsyncMock` of `GitHubClient`) from `conftest.py`. Run with `pytest tests/` (asyncio mode is auto).
